WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.409
After leaving the station, Phileas Fogg quietly returned

2
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:11.805
to his house on Savile Row. His neighbors would have been surprised to learn that he had returned. The

3
00:00:11.806 --> 00:00:17.135
doors and windows of his house remained closed. The building was quiet. Mr. Fogg

4
00:00:17.136 --> 00:00:22.660
was ruined! And by the blundering of a detective! Only a few

5
00:00:22.661 --> 00:00:27.345
pounds were left in the carpetbag that had traveled

6
00:00:27.346 --> 00:00:32.319
the world with him. All that remained of his fortune–twenty-thousand pounds deposited at

7
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.914
the bank–he now owed to his friends at the Reform Club. He set aside a room in

8
00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:42.538
the house for Aouda, who was overwhelmed with grief at her

9
00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:45.004
protector's misfortune. Passepartout was also desperately concerned

10
00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:50.176
for his master. He watched all evening as Mr. Fogg sat

11
00:00:50.177 --> 00:00:55.707
silently in his armchair. Late at night Mr. Fogg climbed the stairs to bed.

12
00:00:55.708 --> 00:01:01.678
Many a ruined man has chosen suicide, and fearful that Mr.

13
00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:07.356
Fogg planned some desperate act, Passepartout waited faithfully all night at his master's door.

14
00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:10.655
In the morning Mr. Fogg called Passepartout into his room

15
00:01:10.656 --> 00:01:17.944
and asked him to look after Aouda's breakfast and lunch. Mr. Fogg would be too busy setting his affairs

16
00:01:17.945 --> 00:01:21.722
in order. In the evening he

17
00:01:21.723 --> 00:01:28.796
would ask permission to have a few moments of conversation with the lady. Passepartout felt so horrible

18
00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:33.054
and responsible that he could contain himself

19
00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:38.880
no longer. "My master! Mr. Fogg!" he cried. "Don't you blame me?

20
00:01:38.881 --> 00:01:43.967
It was my fault that—" "I blame no one," returned Mr. Fogg.

21
00:01:43.968 --> 00:01:47.616
"Go!" Passepartout left Mr. Fogg's room and went to deliver the master's

22
00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:51.797
message to Aouda. Mr. Fogg shut himself in his room and

23
00:01:51.798 --> 00:01:54.857
busied himself at his desk.

24
00:01:54.858 --> 00:02:00.116
Passepartout also had a matter to settle. In the mailbox that

25
00:02:00.117 --> 00:02:03.083
morning, he had found a bill from

26
00:02:03.084 --> 00:02:07.240
the gas company. He remembered at once that the heater

27
00:02:07.241 --> 00:02:10.270
in his room had been burning for eighty days!

28
00:02:10.271 --> 00:02:14.218
He wasted not one minute more and rushed

29
00:02:14.219 --> 00:02:17.253
to his room to shut it off. At

30
00:02:17.254 --> 00:02:23.209
half-past seven that evening, Mr. Fogg knocked on Aouda's door. No emotion was visible on his

31
00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.825
face. He entered and sat several

32
00:02:25.826 --> 00:02:29.843
minutes without speaking. Then he said, "Madam, please forgive me

33
00:02:29.844 --> 00:02:35.597
for bringing you to England. When I decided to bring you so far away from

34
00:02:35.598 --> 00:02:40.996
your country, I was rich. I was going to put a

35
00:02:40.997 --> 00:02:44.838
portion of my fortune at your disposal. Then you

36
00:02:44.839 --> 00:02:49.939
would have been free and happy. But now I

37
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:55.858
am ruined." "I know it, Mr. Fogg," said Aouda. "And I ask in return, will you forgive

38
00:02:55.859 --> 00:03:02.602
me for following you? For, perhaps, delaying you and contributing to your loss." "Madam,

39
00:03:02.603 --> 00:03:09.432
you could not have remained in India. Your safety depended on traveling as far as possible

40
00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:12.174
from those chasing you." "But what

41
00:03:12.175 --> 00:03:18.066
will become of you?" asked Aouda. "I have need of nothing," said Fogg. "Surely

42
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:20.816
poverty will not

43
00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:23.151
overtake a man

44
00:03:23.152 --> 00:03:24.338
like

45
00:03:24.339 --> 00:03:27.574
you," said Aouda. "Your friends—" "They

46
00:03:27.575 --> 00:03:32.739
are not close friends," he replied simply. "Your relatives—" "I no

47
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:37.050
longer have any relatives." "Mr. Fogg," said Aouda,

48
00:03:37.051 --> 00:03:41.764
rising. "May I offer you a kinswoman and a friend? Will you

49
00:03:41.765 --> 00:03:46.291
have me for your wife?" At this, Mr. Fogg rose in his

50
00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:54.112
turn. There was a light in his eyes and a slight trembling of his lips. Aouda looked into his face. Fogg

51
00:03:54.113 --> 00:03:59.697
shut his eyes for an instant as if to avoid her look, but when he

52
00:03:59.698 --> 00:04:04.090
opened them again, he said, "I love you. Yes, by all

53
00:04:04.091 --> 00:04:07.579
that is holiest, I love you, and

54
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:11.026
I am entirely yours." "Ah!" cried Aouda, pressing

55
00:04:11.027 --> 00:04:17.034
his hand to her heart. Passepartout was summoned and appeared immediately. Mr. Fogg still

56
00:04:17.035 --> 00:04:21.101
held Aouda's hand in his own, and Passepartout immediately

57
00:04:21.102 --> 00:04:26.220
understood what had happened. Passepartout's face became as radiant as the

58
00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:29.664
tropical sun at its highest. Mr. Fogg asked

59
00:04:29.665 --> 00:04:35.928
him if it was not too late to notify the Reverend Samuel Wilson that very evening.

60
00:04:35.929 --> 00:04:39.670
Passepartout smiled one of his warmest smiles and said,

61
00:04:39.671 --> 00:04:43.067
"It is never too late!" It was five minutes

62
00:04:43.068 --> 00:04:47.734
past eight in the evening. "Will the wedding be tomorrow?"

63
00:04:47.735 --> 00:04:53.102
asked Passepartout. "Tomorrow," said Mr. Fogg, turning to Aouda. "Monday." "Yes, Monday," she replied. Passepartout

64
00:04:53.103 --> 00:04:55.874
hurried off as fast as

65
00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:59.062
his legs could carry him. Passepartout's Guidebook At

66
00:04:59.063 --> 00:05:02.660
least something good came out of our trip.

67
00:05:02.661 --> 00:05:08.546
Mr. Fogg and Aouda are in love

68
00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:12.153
and will get married! I must find Reverend Wilson

69
00:05:12.154 --> 00:05:17.686
and start preparing for the wedding. There is so much to do! Did you

70
00:05:17.687 --> 00:05:22.079
know that most brides in England wear white? This tradition was started when

71
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.659
Queen Victoria got married in 1840 and wore a white dress. Before that

72
00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:32.681
brides wore just about any color, with red being quite a popular

73
00:05:32.682 --> 00:05:40.767
choice. As for Mr. Fogg, well, he won't be wearing white! He might wear a

74
00:05:40.768 --> 00:05:44.684
frock coat, the long dress coat that is traditional for

75
00:05:44.685 --> 00:05:48.406
Victorian gentlemen. But today many men are wearing morning

76
00:05:48.407 --> 00:05:54.667
coats to weddings. A morning coat is cut short in the front with two long tails in the back.

77
00:05:54.668 --> 00:06:00.620
A morning coat is black or gray. After the church wedding, it is customary to have a

78
00:06:00.621 --> 00:06:05.947
wedding breakfast. I must be sure to reserve a special corner in Mr. Fogg's

79
00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:11.818
house where Aouda can receive the guests who come to congratulate

80
00:06:11.819 --> 00:06:15.493
the happy couple. After the reception, perhaps Mr. Fogg

81
00:06:15.494 --> 00:06:18.569
and Aouda will go on a honeymoon.

82
00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:30.356
Please forgive me, readers, if I do not linger much longer. Why, I have a wedding to plan!

